From c62c98ab5f944ecb243bc5367fdb81a2b713d2ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:13:12 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] perf: wait for input instead of polling source and target
 devices

Every source device polled its device every 2.5 ms and every target
device ran on a 1-16 ms interval, whether or not anything happened. On
a handheld with one gamepad (a MANGMI Air X: two evdev sources, an
X-Box Elite, keyboard, mouse, touchpad and DBus target) that is about
1,550 wakeups a second with no input at all.

Sources: a SourceInputDevice can name a file descriptor that becomes
readable when poll() has events (wake_fd()). The evdev gamepad,
keyboard and touchscreen sources name their device. Their SourceDriver
then waits on that fd and on an eventfd the SourceDeviceClient signals
after each command (output events such as rumble included), instead of
sleeping out the poll rate. While the translator has scheduled events
(has_scheduled_events()) it polls at the poll rate as before. Sources
without a wake fd (hidraw, iio, ...) are unchanged.

Targets: a TargetOutputDevice says whether it needs the periodic poll
now (needs_periodic_poll(), true by default) and can name a file
descriptor that becomes readable when poll() has output events
(output_fd()). The xpad target names its uinput device, so force
feedback uploads and plays are read when they arrive, and needs the
interval only while it has queued events; keyboard and DBus never need
it, the mouse only while it moves and the touchpad only while touched.
A target that needs neither waits only for commands, its output fd and
scheduled events. The others keep their interval.

The mouse resets its time delta when it starts moving, since poll() no
longer runs while it rests.
